          "type": "READING",
          "title": "Who Gets to Decide?",
          "content": {
            "text": "A student-run account documenting incidents of bullying at a large secondary school was suspended last year after several complaints that its anonymous posts amounted to harassment of the staff and students named in them. Supporters called the suspension censorship, plain and simple; the school called it a necessary response to posts that had, in a handful of cases, triggered genuine threats against the people they named. Both descriptions, uncomfortably, were at least partly accurate.\n\nIt is tempting to treat free speech online as an absolute principle that should never yield to any competing concern. That position has real force: platforms genuinely have, on numerous documented occasions, removed political speech, journalism and legitimate dissent under the broad and convenient justification of 'moderation', sometimes at the quiet request of the very governments such speech was criticising. Dismissing every concern about censorship as paranoid overreaction would be a serious mistake.\n\nThat said, treating free speech as though it carried no corresponding limits produces its own, equally serious, problems. Inflammatory content, left entirely unmoderated, does not simply coexist peacefully alongside more measured voices; it tends, by design and by algorithm, to spread further and faster than calmer, more careful speech ever could. However strongly one values open debate, it is difficult to defend a system in which coordinated harassment campaigns enjoy exactly the same protection as considered argument, merely because both technically qualify as 'speech'.\n\nGranted, the line between harmful content and merely unpopular opinion is drawn differently by almost everyone who is asked to draw it, which is precisely why moderation decisions provoke such intense and recurring controversy. Even so, the difficulty of drawing a line perfectly is not, by itself, a persuasive argument for refusing to draw one at all; few rules in any domain of life are applied with total consistency, and that imperfection is rarely treated as sufficient reason to abandon the rule entirely.\n\nCritics of heavy-handed moderation are right to insist that accountability for these decisions remains strikingly weak: users are frequently unable to appeal a removal meaningfully, and the precise rules being applied are often vague, inconsistently enforced, or simply undisclosed. Platforms, for their part, counter that total transparency would let bad actors reverse-engineer the rules and evade them more effectively, an argument that has some merit but conveniently also shields platforms from the scrutiny their critics are demanding.\n\nWhat should be resisted, on either side of this argument, is the comfortable fiction that one's own preferred position carries no costs whatsoever. Looser moderation protects legitimate dissent but tolerates real harassment and harm; stricter moderation reduces that harm but hands considerable, largely unaccountable power to whoever is doing the moderating. Whichever balance a given platform eventually settles on, it will not please everyone, and it probably should not, since a rule that satisfied every side of this particular argument would most likely not be doing very much at all."
          }
